<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Panini</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 5th–4th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Pāṇini</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Panini was an ancient Indian grammarian, probably active in the 5th or 4th century BCE in the north-west of the subcontinent, traditionally associated with Shalatula near Gandhara. His Ashtadhyayi, a treatise of nearly 4,000 rules, gives a complete and highly compressed description of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> grammar. Its incidental mentions of peoples and places make it a valuable source for the early states and republics of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p></div>
